From c7b63aaadbe994051439af7cb8616d0a10ebd6f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crindo18 <chrisjohnalado@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 06:47:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] M5: open-set threshold tuning, exact McNemar, and a
 system-labelling fix

BLOCKING FIX. s03_enroll and s06_runEvaluation were hard-coded to
enhancedConfig() while a separate s06_system variable carried the label
into the filename, the 'system' column and every downstream figure.
Setting s06_system='baseline' would have written results_baseline_*.csv
stamped system=baseline containing ENHANCED numbers - and a McNemar test
between two such files compares the enhanced system against itself and
reports no significant difference, which looks exactly like a real
negative result. New systemConfig(name) derives the config FROM the
label so the two cannot disagree.

M5 implementations:
  mcnemarTest      exact two-sided binomial on discordant pairs, via
                   gammaln so large m does not overflow. Concordant
                   pairs verified not to move p - that property is what
                   makes the test paired and it is easy to break.
  tuneThresholds   2-D sweep of (tau, rho); default objective is max
                   recall subject to a FAR budget, because naming the
                   wrong song is worse than saying nothing. Refuses any
                   row not from the dev split. Tunes on the proposal's
                   declared envelope (0 dB and above) by default, so the
                   threshold is not chosen by cells no system can serve.
  plotOpenSetRoc   recall vs FAR with the Pareto frontier highlighted -
                   two thresholds mean the sweep is a cloud, not a
                   curve, and a dominated point must not read as one.
  s05_tuneThresholds  tunes both systems, shows what other FAR budgets
                   would buy, prints the config lines to paste back.

Also correct in the M4-check commit and kept: enrolment now forces
denoise off, so references are never denoised (blueprint 3.7). The
fingerprint cfgTag is unaffected because extractCfg.tag is not
recomputed, so the index tag check still passes.

tOpenSetTuning: 12 tests covering the exact p-value against its closed
form, paired-ness, the dev-split guard, FAR-budget compliance, FAR
monotonicity in tau, and label/config agreement.
